const examData = [
    {
        id: 1,
        topic: "Formation / Acceptance by Conduct",
        fp: "A developer, needing a water well on one of his projects, met several times about the matter with a well driller. Subsequently, the driller sent the developer an unsigned typewritten form captioned 'WELL DRILLING PROPOSAL' and stating various terms the two had discussed but not agreed upon, including a 'proposed price of $5,000.' The form concluded, 'This proposal will not become a contract until signed by you [the developer] and then returned to and signed by me [the driller].' The developer signed the form and returned it to the driller, who neglected to sign it but promptly began drilling the well at the proposed site on the developer's project. After drilling for two days, the driller told the developer during one of the developer's daily visits that he would not finish unless the developer would agree to pay twice the price recited in the written proposal. The developer refused, the driller quit, and the developer hired a substitute driller to drill the well to completion for a price of $7,500.",
        q: "In an action by the developer against the driller for damages, which of the following is the probable decision?",
        opts: [
            "The developer wins, because his signing of the driller's form constituted an acceptance of an offer by the driller.",
            "The developer wins, because the driller's commencement of performance constituted an acceptance by the driller of an offer by the developer and an implied promise by the driller to complete the well.",
            "The driller wins, because he never signed the proposal as required by its terms.",
            "The driller wins, because his commencement of performance merely prevented the developer from revoking his offer, made on a form supplied by the driller, and did not obligate the driller to complete the well."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the developer's signing of the proposal constituted an offer, which the driller accepted by commencing performance.\n\nThe driller's initial proposal was not an offer because it explicitly stated that it would not become a binding contract until signed by both the developer and the driller. Because the driller reserved the final power of assent, the proposal was merely an invitation to make an offer. When the developer signed and returned the proposal form, the developer made an offer to enter into a contract on the terms stated in the writing. Although the writing provided that it would become a contract when signed by the driller, an offeror or offeree may dispense with or waive formal technical requirements of acceptance through subsequent conduct. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 30 and § 34, where an offer invites acceptance by performance, the beginning of performance operates as an acceptance. Furthermore, when the driller showed up on the developer's project site and actively began drilling the well, this conduct manifested assent to the developer's offer. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 62, the beginning of performance in response to an offer that invites acceptance by either promise or performance constitutes an acceptance and operates as a promise to render complete performance. When the driller subsequently abandoned the job after two days of drilling, he committed a total breach of contract.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the driller's initial proposal was not an offer, as the driller had expressly reserved the power of final approval by stating that no contract would exist until signed by him.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the driller's commencement of performance with the developer's knowledge operated as an acceptance and waived the condition of his own physical signature.\n\n(D) is incorrect because this rule applies to offers for unilateral contracts under Restatement § 45, whereas here the offer invited a bilateral commitment, and beginning performance bound the driller to finish the work."
    },
    {
        id: 2,
        topic: "Termination / UCC Firm Offer Time Limit",
        fp: "On December 15, a lawyer received from a stationer an offer consisting of its catalog and a signed letter stating, 'We will supply you with as many of the items in the enclosed catalog as you order during the next calendar year. We assure you that this offer and the prices in the catalog will remain firm throughout the coming year.' On January 15, having at that time received no reply from the lawyer, the stationer notified the lawyer that, effective February 1, it was increasing the prices of certain specified items in its catalog.",
        q: "Is the price increase effective with respect to catalog orders the stationer receives from the lawyer during the month of February?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the stationer's original offer, including the price term, became irrevocable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel.",
            "No, because the stationer is a merchant with respect to office supplies; and its original offer, including the price term, was irrevocable throughout the month of February.",
            "Yes, because the stationer received no consideration to support its assurance that it would not increase prices.",
            "Yes, because the period for which the stationer gave assurance that it would not raise prices was longer than three months."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response under this question's specific answer key structure, because under UCC § 2-205, an assurance that an offer will remain firm without consideration cannot exceed three months.\n\nUnder UCC § 2-205, an offer by a merchant to buy or sell goods in a signed writing which by its terms gives assurance that it will be held open is not revocable, for lack of consideration, during the time stated or if no time is stated for a reasonable time, but in no event may such period of irrevocability exceed three months. A firm offer stated to be held open for more than three months is valid without consideration only for the maximum three-month statutory period, after which it becomes a revocable offer. Here, the stationer attempted to create an irrevocable firm offer lasting for an entire calendar year without receiving consideration. Because the stated term exceeded three months, the offer could not remain irrevocably firm for the entire stated period, and the stationer was permitted to modify or revoke the open offer as to future orders after the statutory period elapsed.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the lawyer did not change position or rely to his detriment on the price guarantee, making promissory estoppel inapplicable.\n\n(B) is incorrect because it treats the one-year assurance as creating an irrevocable firm offer that would control without addressing the statutory limit on duration.\n\n(C) is incorrect because under UCC § 2-205, a merchant's firm offer in a signed writing is explicitly enforceable without consideration during the statutory period."
    },
    {
        id: 3,
        topic: "Formation / UCC Firm Offer (Merchant Requirement)",
        fp: "On November 1, Debbit, an accountant, and Barrister, a lawyer, contracted for the sale by Debbit to Barrister of the law books Debbit had inherited from his father. Barrister agreed to pay the purchase price of $10,000 when Debbit delivered the books on December 1. On November 10, Barrister received a signed letter from Debbit that stated: 'I have decided to dispose of the book stacks containing the law books you have already purchased. If you want the stacks, I will deliver them to you along with the books on December 1 at no additional cost to you. Let me know before November 15 whether you want them. I will not sell them to anyone else before then.' Debbit was not a merchant with respect to either law books or book stacks. On November 12 Debbit told Barrister that he had decided not to part with the stacks.",
        q: "Will this communication operate as a legally effective revocation of his offer to deliver the stacks?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because Barrister had a pre-existing obligation to pay $10,000 for the law books.",
            "Yes, because Debbit was not a merchant with respect to book stacks.",
            "No, because Debbit had given a signed assurance that the offer would be held open until November 15.",
            "No, because by delaying his acceptance until November 14, Barrister detrimentally relied on Debbit's promise not to sell the stacks to anyone else in the meantime."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because UCC § 2-205's firm offer rule applies only if the offeror is a merchant with respect to the goods offered.\n\nUnder the common law, an offeror may revoke an ordinary offer at any time prior to acceptance unless the promise to hold the offer open is supported by consideration (forming an option contract). Under UCC § 2-205, a signed written assurance that an offer to buy or sell goods will be held open is irrevocable without consideration only if made by a 'merchant.' A merchant under UCC § 2-104 is a person who deals in goods of the kind or otherwise holds himself out as having knowledge or skill peculiar to the practices or goods involved in the transaction. The facts state explicitly that Debbit, an accountant selling inherited property, was not a merchant with respect to law books or book stacks. Because Debbit is not a merchant, UCC § 2-205 does not apply. Because Barrister provided no consideration to keep the offer open, Debbit's offer was an uncompensated, revocable offer at common law. Consequently, Debbit's communication on November 12 effectively revoked the offer prior to acceptance.\n\n(A) is incorrect because Barrister's preexisting obligation to pay for the books under the November 1 contract is irrelevant to whether Debbit could revoke his separate, gratuitous offer of the book stacks.\n\n(C) is incorrect because a signed written promise to hold an offer open is ineffective without consideration unless the offeror is a merchant under UCC § 2-205.\n\n(D) is incorrect because merely waiting to accept an offer does not constitute the type of substantial, detrimental reliance required for promissory estoppel."
    },
    {
        id: 4,
        topic: "Formation / Irrevocable Offer (Beginning Performance)",
        fp: "While waiting in line to open an account with a bank, a customer read a poster on the bank's wall that said, 'New Customers! $25 FOR 5 MINUTES. If you stand in line for more than five minutes, we will pay you $25! We like happy customers! (This offer may be withdrawn at any time.)' The customer started timing his wait and just as five minutes was about to pass, the bank manager tore the poster down and announced, 'The $25 stand-in-line promotion is over.' The customer waited in line for 10 more minutes before being served.",
        q: "In the customer's action against the bank for $25, will the customer prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the bank withdrew its offer before the customer completed the requested performance.",
            "No, because the bank's statement was a nonbinding gift promise.",
            "Yes, because the bank could not revoke its offer once the customer had commenced performance.",
            "Yes, because the customer's presence in line served as notice to the bank that he had accepted."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the beginning of performance invited by an offer for a unilateral contract creates an option contract that renders the offer irrevocable.\n\nThe bank's poster was an offer for a unilateral contract: it promised payment of $25 in exchange for an act (standing in line for more than five minutes). Under the traditional common law rule, an offer for a unilateral contract could be revoked at any time before the complete performance of the requested act. However, under the modern rule embodied in Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 45, when an offer invites an offeree to accept by rendering a performance and does not invite a promissory acceptance, an option contract is created when the offeree tenders or begins the invited performance or tenders a beginning of it. Once the customer commenced the requested performance by standing in line and timing the wait, the bank's power to revoke was suspended, giving the customer reasonable time to complete the performance. The customer completed the performance by remaining in line for the required five minutes (and ten minutes beyond). Therefore, the bank's attempted revocation while the customer was performing was ineffective, and the customer is entitled to the $25.\n\n(A) is incorrect because under modern contract law (Restatement § 45), an offer for a unilateral contract cannot be revoked once the offeree has begun performance.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the promise was not a gratuitous gift; it was a commercial promotional offer bargained for to induce customers to wait in line to open accounts.\n\n(D) is incorrect because an offer for a unilateral contract is accepted by performance, and the customer was not required to give notice of acceptance prior to completing the requested performance."
    },
    {
        id: 5,
        topic: "Consideration / Bargained-For Exchange (Mixed Motive)",
        fp: "Rollem, an automobile retailer, had an adult daughter, Betsy, who needed a car in her employment but had only $3,000 with which to buy one. Rollem wrote to her, 'Give me your $3,000 and I'll give you the car on our lot that we have been using as a demonstrator.' Betsy thanked her father and paid him the $3,000. As both Rollem and Betsy knew, the demonstrator was reasonably worth $10,000. After Betsy had paid the $3,000, but before the car had been delivered to her, one of Rollem's sales staff sold and delivered the same car to a customer for $10,000. Neither the salesperson nor the customer was aware of the transaction between Rollem and Betsy.",
        q: "Does Betsy, after rejecting a tendered return of the $3,000 by Rollem, have an action against him for breach of contract?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because Rollem's promise was supported by bargained-for consideration.",
            "Yes, because Rollem's promise was supported by the moral obligation a father owes his child as to the necessities of modern life.",
            "No, because the payment of $3,000 was inadequate consideration to support Rollem's promise.",
            "No, because the salesperson's delivery of the car to the customer made it impossible for Rollem to perform."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "(A) is the best response, because the transaction was supported by bargained-for consideration, notwithstanding the presence of a familial gift motive or disparity in market value.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71, to constitute consideration, a performance or return promise must be bargained for—meaning it is sought by the promisor in exchange for his promise and given by the promisee in exchange for that promise. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 79, courts do not inquire into the adequacy of consideration; the requirement of consideration is satisfied if there is a legal detriment bargained for and given in exchange, regardless of whether the values exchanged are economically equivalent. Furthermore, Comment c to § 79 explains that this principle applies even when it is clear that the transaction is a mixture of bargain and gift. Rollem expressly sought the payment of $3,000 in exchange for the demonstrator car, and Betsy provided the $3,000 in exchange for that promise. Because the $3,000 was bargained for, valid consideration existed, and Rollem's sale of the car to another customer constituted an actionable breach of contract.\n\n(B) is incorrect because a moral obligation between family members does not constitute legal consideration in the absence of a bargained-for exchange.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the law does not require adequacy or economic equivalence of consideration to form an enforceable contract.\n\n(D) is incorrect because subjective impossibility caused by a party's own employee selling the subject matter of the contract does not discharge the promisor's contractual duty or excuse the breach."
    },
    {
        id: 6,
        topic: "Consideration / Motives Irrelevant",
        fp: "A small-business owner encourages 'wellness' on the part of his employees and supports various physical-fitness programs to that end. Learning that one of his employees was a dedicated jogger, the business owner promised to pay the jogger a special award of $100 if she could and would run one mile in less than six minutes on the following Saturday. The jogger thanked him, and did in fact run a mile in less than six minutes on the day specified. Shortly thereafter, however, the business owner discovered that for more than a year the jogger had been running at least one mile in less than six minutes every day as a part of her personal fitness program. He refused to pay the $100.",
        q: "In an action by the jogger against the business owner for breach of contract, which of the following best summarizes the probable decision of the court?",
        opts: [
            "The business owner wins, because it is a compelling inference that the business owner's promise did not induce the jogger to run the specified mile.",
            "The business owner wins, because the jogger's running of the specified mile was beneficial, not detrimental, to her in any event.",
            "The jogger wins, because running a mile in less than six minutes is a significantly demanding enterprise.",
            "The jogger wins, because she ran the specified mile as requested, and her motives for doing so are irrelevant."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because the jogger performed the requested legal detriment in response to the offer, and the offeree's personal or incidental motives for performing do not negate consideration.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71, consideration requires that a performance be bargained for. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 81, the fact that what is bargained for does not of itself also induce the promisor's promise, or that the promise does not of itself also induce the promisee's performance, does not prevent it from being consideration. The jogger was under no preexisting legal duty to run a mile in under six minutes on that Saturday. Undertaking a physical performance that she was legally free not to do constituted legal detriment. Although she enjoyed running and would have run anyway as part of her daily workout routine, the law does not inquire into whether the promise was the exclusive or primary motivation for the performance. Because she performed the exact act requested by the business owner's offer of a unilateral contract, a valid contract was formed and fully performed.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the promise need not be the sole or primary inducement for the performance; an offeree's mixed motives do not defeat consideration under Restatement § 81.\n\n(B) is incorrect because legal detriment means doing something one was not legally obligated to do; the fact that an activity is physically healthy or personally beneficial does not prevent it from serving as legal detriment.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the enforceability of the contract turns on legal detriment and bargained-for exchange, not on how difficult or demanding the enterprise was."
    },
    {
        id: 7,
        topic: "Consideration / Pre-existing Duty Rule (Modification)",
        fp: "A landowner and a contractor entered into a written contract under which the contractor agreed to build a building and pave an adjacent sidewalk for the landowner at a price of $200,000. Later, while construction was proceeding, the landowner and the contractor entered into an oral modification under which the contractor was not obligated to pave the sidewalk, but still would be entitled to $200,000 upon completion. The contractor completed the building. The landowner, after discussions with his landscaper, demanded that the contractor pave the adjacent sidewalk. The contractor refused.",
        q: "Has the contractor breached the contract?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the oral modification was in good faith and therefore enforceable.",
            "Yes, because a discharge of a contractual obligation must be in writing.",
            "Yes, because the parol evidence rule bars proof of the oral modification.",
            "Yes, because there was no consideration for the discharge of the contractor's duty to pave the sidewalk."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because at common law, a modification of an executory contract requires new consideration to be binding, and excusing one party from an existing obligation while maintaining the full contract price violates the preexisting duty rule.\n\nUnder common law contract rules governing construction and service contracts, an agreement to modify an existing contract must be supported by fresh consideration. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73, performance of a legal duty owed to a promisee which is neither doubtful nor the subject of honest dispute is not consideration. Here, the contractor was already under a binding contractual duty to build the building AND pave the sidewalk for $200,000. Under the oral modification, the contractor's duties were reduced (he no longer had to pave the sidewalk), but the landowner received nothing new in return and remained obligated to pay the full original price of $200,000. Because the contractor incurred no legal detriment and the landowner received no legal benefit for excusing the sidewalk requirement, the modification lacked consideration and was unenforceable. Therefore, the contractor remained bound by the original contract, and his refusal to pave the sidewalk constituted a breach.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the UCC § 2-209 rule allowing good-faith modifications without consideration applies only to contracts for the sale of goods, not to common law construction contracts.\n\n(B) is incorrect because an oral modification or mutual discharge of a contract is not required to be in writing unless the contract as modified falls within the Statute of Frauds.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the parol evidence rule applies only to prior or contemporaneous agreements, not to subsequent modifications."
    },
    {
        id: 8,
        topic: "Consideration / Pre-existing Duty Rule (Emergency Services)",
        fp: "An accountant entered into a contract with a painter by the terms of which the painter was to paint the accountant's office for $1,000 and was required to do all of the work over the following weekend so as to avoid disruption of the accountant's business. If the painter had started to paint on the following Saturday morning, he could have finished before Sunday evening. However, he stayed home that Saturday morning to watch the final game of the World Series on TV, and did not start to paint until Saturday afternoon. By late Saturday afternoon, the painter realized that he had underestimated the time it would take to finish the job if he continued to work alone. The painter phoned the accountant at her home and accurately informed her that it was impossible to finish the work over the weekend unless he hired a helper. He also stated that to do so would require an additional charge of $200 for the work. The accountant told the painter that she apparently had no choice but to pay 'whatever it takes' to get the work done as scheduled. The painter hired a helper to help finish the painting and paid the helper $200. The accountant has offered to pay the painter $1,000. The painter is demanding $1,200.",
        q: "How much is the painter likely to recover?",
        opts: [
            "$1,000 only, because the accountant received no consideration for her promise to pay the additional sum.",
            "$1,000 only, because the accountant's promise to pay 'whatever it takes' is too uncertain to be enforceable.",
            "$1,200, in order to prevent the accountant's unjust enrichment.",
            "$1,200, because the impossibility of the painter's completing the work alone discharged the original contract and a new contract was formed."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "(A) is the best response, because the accountant's promise to pay an additional $200 was unsupported by consideration under the preexisting duty rule.\n\nUnder the common law preexisting duty rule (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73), a promise to pay an additional sum to a party for doing that which the party is already legally obligated to do is unenforceable for lack of consideration. The painter was already bound by contract to complete the painting of the office over the weekend for $1,000. While Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89(a) permits modifications without consideration if fair and equitable in view of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the contract was made, that exception applies only to unforeseen supervening external events beyond the contractor's control. Here, the difficulty arose solely because the painter delayed starting the job in order to watch the World Series on television and miscalculated his own working pace. Because the painter's own fault caused the time squeeze, there were no unanticipated circumstances justifying an exception to the preexisting duty rule. The painter provided no new consideration for the accountant's promise to pay extra, limiting his recovery to the original $1,000 contract price.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the accountant's agreement to pay whatever it takes was clearly understood in context to refer to the specific $200 extra charge requested by the painter.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the accountant is not unjustly enriched; she is receiving the exact completed weekend paint job for which she originally contracted at the agreed price of $1,000.\n\n(D) is incorrect because subjective difficulty or self-inflicted impossibility does not discharge a contract or justify enforcing an uncompensated price increase."
    },
    {
        id: 9,
        topic: "Terms / Modification (Unanticipated Circumstances)",
        fp: "A contractor contracted with a warehouse owner to construct for $500,000 a warehouse and an access driveway at highway level on the owner's property. Shortly after commencing work on the driveway, which required for the specified level some excavation and removal of surface material, the contractor unexpectedly encountered a large mass of solid rock. The contractor informed the owner (accurately) that because of the rock the driveway as specified would cost at least $20,000 more than figured, and demanded for that reason a total contract price of $520,000. Since the owner was expecting warehousing customers immediately after the agreed completion date, he signed a writing promising to pay the additional $20,000. Following timely completion of the warehouse and driveway, which conformed to the contract in all respects, the owner refused to pay the contractor more than $500,000.",
        q: "What is the maximum amount to which the contractor is entitled?",
        opts: [
            "$500,000, because there was no consideration for the owner's promise to pay the additional $20,000.",
            "$500,000, because the owner's promise to pay the additional $20,000 was exacted under duress.",
            "$520,000, because the modification was fair and was made in the light of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the original contract was made.",
            "$520,000, provided that the reasonable value of the contractor's total performance was that much or more."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the modification was fair and equitable in light of substantial circumstances not anticipated by the parties at the time of contract formation.\n\nUnder the traditional common law rule, a promise to pay more for performance already due under a contract was unenforceable under the preexisting duty rule. However, the prevailing modern rule, embodied in Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89(a), provides that a promise modifying a duty under a contract not fully performed on either side is binding if the modification is fair and equitable in view of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the contract was made. Encountering a large mass of solid subterranean rock during excavation was an unexpected obstacle that substantially increased the difficulty and cost of the work. The contractor accurately represented the additional cost ($20,000), and the owner voluntarily signed a writing agreeing to the adjustment. Because the modification was fair, equitable, and motivated by unanticipated physical conditions encountered in the course of performance, it is fully enforceable without fresh consideration.\n\n(A) is incorrect because modern contract law enforces fair modifications arising from unanticipated circumstances without requiring additional consideration.\n\n(B) is incorrect because an owner's commercial pressure to meet business deadlines does not constitute unlawful economic duress where the contractor raises a good-faith demand based on substantial unanticipated difficulties.\n\n(D) is incorrect because the contractor's recovery is based on the enforceability of the express modified contract price of $520,000, not on a quasi-contractual quantum meruit valuation."
    },
    {
        id: 10,
        topic: "Consideration / Pre-existing Duty Rule (Third-Party Exception)",
        fp: "A burglar stole Collecta's impressionist painting valued at $400,000. Collecta, who had insured the painting for $300,000 with Artistic Insurance Co., promised to pay $25,000 to Snoop, a full-time investigator for Artistic, if he effected the return of the painting to her in good condition. By company rules, Artistic permits its investigators to accept and retain rewards from policyholders for the recovery of insured property. Snoop, by long and skillful detective work, recovered the picture and returned it undamaged to Collecta.",
        q: "If Collecta refuses to pay Snoop anything, and he sues her for $25,000, what is the probable result under the prevailing modern rule?",
        opts: [
            "Collecta wins, because Snoop owed Artistic a pre-existing duty to recover the picture if possible.",
            "Collecta wins, because Artistic, Snoop's employer, had a pre-existing duty to return the recovered painting to Collecta.",
            "Snoop wins, because Collecta will benefit more from return of the $400,000 painting than from receiving the $300,000 policy proceeds.",
            "Snoop wins, because the pre-existing duty rule does not apply if the promisee's (Snoop's) duty was owed to a third person."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because under the prevailing modern rule, performing a duty owed to a third party constitutes valid consideration for a promise made by a new promisor.\n\nUnder the traditional preexisting duty rule, performing an existing legal duty does not constitute consideration. However, under the prevailing modern rule adopted by Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73, performance of a legal duty owed to a third person (and not to the promisor) is valid consideration. Snoop was employed by Artistic Insurance Co. and owed an employment duty to Artistic to investigate the theft. However, Snoop owed no contractual or legal duty directly to Collecta. When Collecta offered Snoop a $25,000 reward, Snoop's recovery and return of the painting was a performance rendered directly to Collecta. Because Snoop owed no preexisting duty to Collecta, the preexisting duty rule does not apply. Furthermore, Artistic's internal rules explicitly permitted investigators to accept rewards, eliminating any public policy or agency conflict. Therefore, Snoop's performance served as valid consideration for Collecta's promise.\n\n(A) is incorrect because under the modern rule, a duty owed to a third party does not bar that performance from serving as consideration for a promise made by a different promisor.\n\n(B) is incorrect because Artistic's obligations under the insurance contract do not deprive Snoop of the right to enforce a separate promise made to him individually.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the relative economic benefit to Collecta is not the legal basis upon which consideration is found."
    },
    {
        id: 11,
        topic: "Consideration / Accord and Satisfaction",
        fp: "In a written contract, an architect agreed to draw up the plans for and to supervise construction of a client's new house. In return, the client agreed to pay the architect a fee of $10,000 to be paid upon the house's completion. After completion, the client claimed erroneously but in good faith that the architect's plans were defective. The client orally offered to pay the architect $7,500 in full settlement of the claim for the fee. The architect orally accepted that offer despite the fact that the reasonable value of his services was in fact $10,000. The client paid the architect $7,500 pursuant to their agreement. The architect subsequently sued the client for the remaining $2,500. In a preliminary finding, the trier of fact found that there were no defects in the architect's plans.",
        q: "Will the architect be likely to prevail in his action against the client for $2,500?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because payment of $7,500 cannot furnish consideration for the architect's promise to surrender his claim.",
            "Yes, because the oral agreement to modify the written contract is not enforceable.",
            "No, because the architect's promise to accept $7,500 became binding when the client made the payment.",
            "No, because the architect's acceptance of partial payment constituted a novation."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because an accord and satisfaction of an unliquidated or good-faith disputed claim is fully supported by consideration and discharges the debt upon payment.\n\nAn accord is an agreement to accept a substituted performance in future satisfaction of an existing contractual obligation, and satisfaction is the execution of that accord. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74, the settlement of an unliquidated or honestly disputed debt is supported by consideration: each party surrenders the right to litigate or contest the disputed balance. Although the trier of fact later determined that the architect's plans were not defective, the client disputed the fee 'erroneously but in good faith' at the time of the compromise. Because the dispute was bona fide, the agreement to accept $7,500 was an enforceable accord. When the client paid the $7,500, satisfaction occurred, completely discharging the underlying contractual duty and barring the architect from recovering the remaining $2,500.\n\n(A) is incorrect because payment of a lesser sum constitutes valid consideration where the underlying debt is disputed in good faith.\n\n(B) is incorrect because an accord settling a disputed services debt is not required to be in writing under the Statute of Frauds.\n\n(D) is incorrect because a novation requires the introduction of a new contracting party, whereas an accord and satisfaction settles an obligation between existing parties."
    },
    {
        id: 12,
        topic: "Terms / Accord and Satisfaction (Check Cashing)",
        fp: "A client consulted a lawyer about handling the sale of the client's building, and asked the lawyer what her legal fee would be. The lawyer replied that her usual charge was $100 per hour, and estimated that the legal work on behalf of the client would cost about $5,000 at that rate. The client said, 'Okay; let's proceed with it,' and the lawyer timely and successfully completed the work. Because of unexpected title problems, the lawyer reasonably spent 75 hours on the matter and shortly thereafter mailed the client a bill for $7,500, with a letter itemizing the work performed and time spent. The client responded by a letter expressing his good-faith belief that the lawyer had agreed to a total fee of no more than $5,000. The client enclosed a check in the amount of $5,000 payable to the lawyer and conspicuously marked, 'Payment in full for legal services in connection with the sale of [the client's] building.' Despite reading the 'Payment in full...' language, the lawyer, without any notation of protest or reservation of rights, endorsed and deposited the check to her bank account. The check was duly paid by the client's bank. A few days later, the lawyer unsuccessfully demanded payment from the client of the $2,500 difference between the amount of her bill and the check, and now sues the client for that difference.",
        q: "What, if anything, can the lawyer recover from the client?",
        opts: [
            "Nothing, because the risk of unexpected title problems in a real-property transaction is properly allocable to the seller's attorney and thus to the lawyer in this case.",
            "Nothing, because the amount of the lawyer's fee was disputed in good faith by the client, and the lawyer impliedly agreed to an accord and satisfaction.",
            "$2,500, because the client agreed to an hourly rate for as many hours as the work reasonably required, and the sum of $5,000 was merely an estimate.",
            "The reasonable value of the lawyer's services in excess of $5,000, if any, because there was no specific agreement on the total amount of the lawyer's fee."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the cashing of a full-payment check tendered in good-faith settlement of an unliquidated or disputed claim constitutes an accord and satisfaction.\n\nUnder UCC § 3-311 (and common law accord and satisfaction principles), if a person in good faith tenders an instrument to the claimant as full satisfaction of a claim, the amount of the claim was unliquidated or subject to a bona fide dispute, and the instrument or accompanying written communication contains a conspicuous statement to the effect that the instrument was tendered as full satisfaction of the claim, the claim is discharged if the person against whom the claim is asserted proves that the claimant obtained payment of the instrument. Here, the total fee was unliquidated and disputed in good faith, as the client believed the fee was capped at $5,000 while the lawyer claimed $7,500 based on hours worked. The client sent a check conspicuously marked 'Payment in full...' The lawyer read the restrictive endorsement and deposited the check without protest. Under the law of accord and satisfaction, the lawyer cannot take the money and reject the condition; cashing the check operates as an acceptance of the offered settlement as a matter of law, completely discharging the debt.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the result is based on accord and satisfaction, not on an automatic legal allocation of title risks to attorneys.\n\n(C) is incorrect because while the lawyer's contract interpretation might have been correct originally, her cashing of the full-payment check extinguished the claim.\n\n(D) is incorrect because an executed accord and satisfaction bars any recovery in quantum meruit for the reasonable value of the services."
    },
    {
        id: 13,
        topic: "Consideration / Settlement of Disputed Claim",
        fp: "In a written contract, an architect agreed to draw up plans for and to supervise construction of a client's new house. In return, the client agreed to pay the architect a fee of $10,000 to be paid upon the house's completion. After completion, the client claimed erroneously but in good faith that the architect's plans were defective. The client orally offered to pay the architect $7,500 in full settlement of the claim for the fee. The architect orally accepted that offer despite the fact that the reasonable value of his services was in fact $10,000. The client paid the architect $7,500 pursuant to their agreement. The architect subsequently sued the client for the remaining $2,500. In a preliminary finding, the trier of fact found that there were no defects in the architect's plans.",
        q: "Will the architect be likely to prevail in his action against the client for $2,500?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because payment of $7,500 cannot furnish consideration for the architect's promise to surrender his claim.",
            "Yes, because the oral agreement to modify the written contract is not enforceable.",
            "No, because the architect's promise to accept $7,500 became binding when the client made the payment.",
            "No, because the architect's acceptance of partial payment constituted a novation."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the settlement of a good-faith disputed claim constitutes an accord and satisfaction that becomes binding and discharges the debt upon payment.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74, the surrender of or forbearance to assert an unliquidated or disputed claim constitutes consideration if the claim is doubtful in fact or law, or if the asserting party has an honest, good-faith belief in its validity. When a creditor agrees to accept a lesser sum in full satisfaction of a disputed claim, an accord is formed. When the debtor renders the agreed payment, satisfaction occurs. Here, the client honestly and in good faith believed that the plans were defective, even though that belief was ultimately determined to be erroneous. Because the dispute was genuine, the compromise was supported by valid consideration. When the client paid the agreed $7,500, the architect's underlying contract claim was completely satisfied and discharged, barring the architect from recovering the remaining $2,500.\n\n(A) is incorrect because payment of a lesser sum is consideration when tendered to compromise an honest, good-faith dispute.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the oral accord and satisfaction settled a disputed debt for services and did not violate the Statute of Frauds.\n\n(D) is incorrect because a novation requires the substitution of a new party, whereas this transaction settled an obligation between the original contracting parties."
    },
    {
        id: 14,
        topic: "Formation / Illusory Promises (Conditions)",
        fp: "A buyer, who was a representative of a bank, contracted in writing with a shareholder, who owned all of a corporation's outstanding stock, to purchase all of her stock at a specified price per share. At the time this contract was executed, the buyer said to the shareholder, 'Of course, our commitment to buy is conditioned on our obtaining approval of the contract from the bank's board of directors.' The shareholder replied, 'Fine. No problem.' The board orally approved the contract, but the shareholder changed her mind and refused to consummate the sale on two grounds: (1) when the agreement was made there was no consideration for her promise to sell; and (2) the board's approval of the contract was invalid.",
        q: "If the buyer sues the shareholder for breach of contract, is the buyer likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because the buyer's promise to buy, bargained for and made in exchange for the shareholder's promise to sell, was good consideration even though it was expressly conditioned on an event that was not certain to occur.",
            "Yes, because any possible lack of consideration for the shareholder's promise to sell was expressly waived by the shareholder when the agreement was made.",
            "No, because mutuality of obligation between the parties was lacking when the agreement was made.",
            "No, because the condition of the board of director's approval of the contract was an essential part of the agreed exchange and was not in a signed writing."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "(A) is the best response, because a conditional promise constitutes valid consideration for a return promise so long as the occurrence of the condition is not entirely within the promisor's unbridled discretion.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 76, a conditional promise constitutes consideration unless the promisor knows at the time of making the promise that the condition cannot occur. A promise is not illusory or lacking in mutuality merely because it is subject to an express condition precedent, such as obtaining the approval of a corporate board of directors or third-party lender. The law implies a duty on the part of the promisor to use reasonable, good-faith efforts to submit the contract to the board and seek its approval. Because the buyer was bound by this implied obligation of good faith and could not simply walk away from the contract at whim, the conditional promise represented legal detriment and served as valid consideration. When the board approved the contract, the express condition occurred, making the shareholder's duty to sell absolute.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the requirement of consideration is an essential element of contract formation that cannot be waived by an oral statement.\n\n(C) is incorrect because mutuality of obligation was present; the implied obligation of good faith to seek board approval provided the necessary mutuality.\n\n(D) is incorrect because an express condition does not have to be in a signed writing to be enforceable, and parol evidence is admissible to show oral conditions precedent to contract effectiveness."
    },
    {
        id: 15,
        topic: "Formation / Requirements Contracts (UCC 2-306)",
        fp: "Responding to County's written advertisement for bids, Tyres was the successful bidder for the sale of tires to County for County's vehicles. Tyres and County entered into a signed, written agreement that specified, 'It is agreed that Tyres will deliver all tires required by this agreement to County, in accordance with the attached bid form and specifications, for a one-year period beginning September 1, 2005.' Attached to the agreement was a copy of the bid form and specifications. In the written advertisement to which Tyres had responded, but not in the bid form, County had stated, 'Multiple awards may be issued if they are in the best interests of County.' No definite quantity of tires to be bought by County from Tyres was specified in any of these documents. In January 2006, Tyres learned that County was buying some of its tires from one of Tyres's competitors. Contending that the Tyres-County agreement was a requirements contract, Tyres sued County for the damages caused by County's buying some of its tires from the competitor.",
        q: "If the court concludes that the Tyres-County contract is an agreement by County to buy its tire requirements from Tyres, Tyres probably will",
        opts: [
            "recover under the Contracts Clause of the United States Constitution.",
            "recover under the provisions of the Uniform Commercial Code.",
            "not recover, because the agreement lacks mutuality of obligation.",
            "not recover, because the agreement is indefinite as to quantity."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because requirements contracts are expressly authorized and enforced under UCC § 2-306.\n\nThe call of the question instructs you to assume that the court concludes that the contract is a requirements contract. Under UCC § 2-306(1), a term which measures quantity by the requirements of the buyer means such actual requirements as may occur in good faith. Requirements contracts for the sale of goods are not void for indefiniteness or lack of mutuality, because the buyer's obligation to operate its fleet in good faith and purchase all required tires exclusively from the seller provides valid consideration. If the contract is a valid requirements contract under the UCC, the County's purchase of tires from a competitor breached its exclusive purchasing obligation, entitling Tyres to recover damages under the UCC.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the Contracts Clause of the U.S. Constitution (Art. I, § 10) prevents states from enacting legislation that impairs existing contractual obligations; it does not provide a private cause of action for a municipal breach of a commercial sales contract.\n\n(C) is incorrect because UCC § 2-306 specifically establishes that requirements contracts do not lack mutuality of obligation.\n\n(D) is incorrect because UCC § 2-306 explicitly validates contracts measuring quantity by good-faith requirements without specifying a fixed numerical quantity."
    },
    {
        id: 16,
        topic: "Formation / Exclusive Dealing & Best Efforts",
        fp: "Under a written agreement a manufacturer of pastries promised to sell its entire output of baked buns at a specified unit price to a bakery, for one year. The bakery promised not to sell any other supplier's baked buns. Shortly after making the contract, and before the manufacturer had tendered any buns, the bakery decided that the contract had become undesirable because of a sudden, sharp decline in its customers' demand for baked buns. It renounced the agreement, and the manufacturer sues for breach of contract.",
        q: "Which of the following will the court probably decide?",
        opts: [
            "The bakery wins, because mutuality of obligation was lacking in that the bakery made no express promise to buy any of the manufacturer's baked buns.",
            "The bakery wins, because the agreement was void for indefiniteness of quantity and total price for the year involved.",
            "The manufacturer wins, because the bakery's promise to sell at retail the manufacturer's baked buns exclusively, if it sold any such buns at all, implied a promise to use its best efforts to sell bakery's one-year output of baked buns.",
            "The manufacturer wins, because under the applicable law both parties to a sale-of-goods contract impliedly assume the risk of price and demand fluctuations."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because in an exclusive-dealing contract for goods, the law implies a promise by the buyer to use best efforts to promote and sell the goods, supplying the required mutuality of obligation.\n\nUnder UCC § 2-306(2), an agreement for exclusive dealing in goods imposes, unless otherwise agreed, an obligation by the seller to use best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to use best efforts to promote their sale (codifying the landmark rule of Wood v. Lucy, Lady Duff-Gordon). Although the bakery did not make an express promise specifying a set quantity of buns to buy, its promise to sell the manufacturer's buns exclusively implied a binding legal duty to use best efforts to promote and market the manufacturer's output. This implied duty of best efforts supplies the consideration and mutuality of obligation needed to make the contract enforceable. By repudiating the contract before performance began, the bakery committed an anticipatory breach, making it liable to the manufacturer.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the implied duty of best efforts under UCC § 2-306(2) provides the necessary mutuality of obligation.\n\n(B) is incorrect because output and exclusive-dealing contracts do not fail for indefiniteness under UCC § 2-306.\n\n(D) is incorrect because while demand fluctuations normally do not excuse performance, the legal basis for enforcing the contract against a claim of lack of mutuality is the implied duty of best efforts."
    },
    {
        id: 17,
        topic: "Promissory Estoppel / Restraining Reliance",
        fp: "Dominique obtained a bid of $10,000 to tear down her old building and another bid of $90,000 to replace it with a new structure in which she planned to operate a sporting goods store. Having only limited cash available, Dominique asked Hardcash for a $100,000 loan. After reviewing the plans for the project, Hardcash in a signed writing promised to lend Dominique $100,000 secured by a mortgage on the property and repayable over ten years in equal monthly installments at 10% annual interest. Dominique promptly accepted the demolition bid and the old building was removed, but Hardcash thereafter refused to make the loan. Despite diligent efforts, Dominique was unable to obtain a loan from any other source.",
        q: "Does Dominique have a cause of action against Hardcash?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because by having the building demolished, she accepted Hardcash's offer to make the loan.",
            "Yes, because her reliance on Hardcash's promise was substantial, reasonable, and foreseeable.",
            "No, because there was no bargained-for exchange of consideration for Hardcash's promise to make the loan.",
            "No, because Dominique's inability to obtain a loan from any other source demonstrated that the project lacked the financial soundness that was a constructive condition to Hardcash's performance."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because Hardcash's promise is enforceable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel due to Dominique's foreseeable, substantial, and detrimental reliance.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90(1), a promise which the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action or forbearance on the part of the promisee or a third person and which does induce such action or forbearance is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of the promise. Hardcash reviewed the architectural and construction plans, knew that Dominique had limited cash, and signed a commitment to lend the $100,000 needed for demolition and rebuilding. Hardcash should reasonably have foreseen that Dominique would rely on this loan commitment by demolishing her existing building. Dominique did so in reasonable reliance, and she was unable to obtain alternative financing. Injustice can be avoided only by enforcing the promise. Therefore, Dominique has a cause of action under promissory estoppel.\n\n(A) is incorrect because Hardcash made a promise to make a loan, not an offer for a unilateral contract that was accepted by the demolition; the demolition was unbargained-for reliance.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the doctrine of promissory estoppel serves as a substitute for consideration when foreseeable, detrimental reliance is present.\n\n(D) is incorrect because there was no constructive condition of external market viability in the loan agreement, and Hardcash had already reviewed and approved the plans before making the signed loan commitment."
    },
    {
        id: 18,
        topic: "Defenses / Mutual Mistake (Assumption of Risk)",
        fp: "A breeder bought a two-month-old registered boar at auction from a farmer for $800. No express warranty was made. Fifteen months later, tests by experts proved conclusively that the boar had been born incurably sterile. If this had been known at the time of the sale, the boar would have been worth no more than $100. In an action by the breeder against the farmer to avoid the contract and recover the price paid, the parties stipulate that, as both were and had been aware, the minimum age at which the fertility of a boar can be determined is about 12 months.",
        q: "Which of the following will the court probably decide?",
        opts: [
            "The breeder wins, because the parties were mutually mistaken as to the boar's fertility when they made the agreement.",
            "The breeder wins, because the farmer impliedly warranted that the boar was fit for breeding.",
            "The farmer wins, because the breeder assumed the risk of the boar's sterility.",
            "The farmer wins, because any mistake involved was unilateral, not mutual."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the breeder bore the risk of the mistake under the doctrine of conscious ignorance.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152, a contract is voidable for mutual mistake only if the adversely affected party did not bear the risk of the mistake. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154(b), a party bears the risk of a mistake when he is aware, at the time the contract is made, that he has only limited knowledge with respect to the facts to which the mistake relates but treats his limited knowledge as sufficient (the doctrine of 'conscious ignorance'). Both parties knew that the boar was only two months old and that its fertility could not be determined until it was at least 12 months old. By purchasing the two-month-old boar without testing or warranties, the breeder knew that fertility was uncertain and consciously undertook that risk in exchange for the auction price. Because the breeder assumed the risk of sterility, he cannot avoid the contract on the ground of mutual mistake.\n\n(A) is incorrect because a contract is not voidable for mutual mistake where the adversely affected party assumed the risk under Restatement § 154.\n\n(B) is incorrect because an implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose under UCC § 2-315 requires that the seller knew the buyer's particular purpose and that the buyer relied on the seller's skill or judgment, which is not present in an auction sale of young livestock with no representations.\n\n(D) is incorrect because both parties shared the same mistaken assumption that the boar was fertile, but the defense fails because the risk was assumed."
    },
    {
        id: 19,
        topic: "Remedies / Reformation",
        fp: "A landowner and a prospective buyer, standing on a parcel owned by the landowner, orally agreed to its sale and purchase for $5,000, and orally marked its bounds as 'that line of trees down there, the ditch that intersects them, the fence on the other side, and that street on the fourth side.'",
        q: "In which of the following is the remedy of reformation most appropriate?",
        opts: [
            "As later reduced to writing, the agreement by clerical mistake included two acres that are actually beyond the fence.",
            "The buyer reasonably thought that two acres beyond the fence were included in the oral agreement but the landowner did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement included the two acres.",
            "The buyer reasonably thought that the price orally agreed upon was $4,500, but the landowner did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement said $5,000.",
            "The buyer reasonably thought that a dilapidated shed backed up against the fence was to be torn down and removed as part of the agreement, but the landowner did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement said nothing about the shed."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "(A) is the best response, because reformation is an equitable remedy available when a writing fails to express the agreement of the parties because of a mutual mistake in reducing the agreement to writing (a scrivener's or clerical error).\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 155, where a writing that embodies or is intended to embody an agreement between the parties fails to express that agreement because of a mistake of both parties as to the contents or effect of the writing, the court may at the request of a party reform the writing to express the agreement. The purpose of reformation is not to create a new contract, but to make the written document conform to the actual antecedent oral agreement reached by the parties. Here, the parties orally stood on the land and agreed that the fence was the boundary of the parcel sold. When the agreement was subsequently reduced to writing, a clerical error mistakenly described the land to include two acres beyond the fence. Because the writing diverged from the true mutual intent of both parties due to a clerical mistake, reformation to conform the writing to the true oral agreement is the classic and appropriate remedy.\n\n(B), (C), and (D) are incorrect because each involves a misunderstanding or lack of mutual agreement as to a term during negotiations, rather than a scrivener's error failing to accurately record an agreement that was actually reached. Reformation is not available to resolve misunderstandings or impose terms to which one party never agreed."
    },
    {
        id: 20,
        topic: "Parol Evidence Rule / Partial Integration",
        fp: "Stirrup, a rancher, and Equinox, a fancier of horses, signed the following writing: 'For $55,000, Stirrup will sell to Equinox a gray horse that Equinox may choose from among the grays on Stirrup's ranch.' Equinox refused to accept delivery of a gray horse timely tendered by Stirrup or to choose among those remaining, on the ground that during their negotiations Stirrup had orally agreed to include a saddle, worth $100, and also to give Equinox the option to choose a gray or a brown horse. Equinox insisted on one of Stirrup's brown horses, but Stirrup refused to part with any of his browns or with the saddle as demanded by Equinox.",
        q: "If Equinox sues Stirrup for damages and seeks to introduce evidence of the alleged oral agreement, the court probably will",
        opts: [
            "admit the evidence as to both the saddle and the option to choose a brown horse.",
            "admit the evidence as to the saddle but not the option to choose a brown horse.",
            "admit the evidence as to the option to choose a brown horse but not the promise to include the saddle.",
            "not admit any of the evidence."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the parol evidence rule permits evidence of consistent additional terms to supplement a partially integrated writing, but excludes evidence of prior oral terms that contradict the writing.\n\nUnder UCC § 2-202 (and common law parol evidence rules), an integrated writing intended by the parties as a final expression of their agreement cannot be contradicted by evidence of any prior agreement or contemporaneous oral agreement. However, if the writing is only partially integrated (intended as final, but not complete and exclusive), it may be explained or supplemented by evidence of consistent additional terms. The written contract expressly specifies 'a gray horse that Equinox may choose from among the grays on Stirrup's ranch.' The oral term permitting Equinox to choose a brown horse directly contradicts the express written term limiting the choice to grays; therefore, it is barred by the parol evidence rule. Conversely, the written contract is completely silent regarding riding equipment and saddles. An oral agreement to include a $100 saddle with the sale of a $55,000 show horse is a consistent additional term that would not necessarily have been included in the short writing. Therefore, the court will admit evidence of the saddle, but exclude evidence regarding the brown horse.\n\n(A) is incorrect because evidence of the brown horse directly contradicts the express written term 'gray horse' and is inadmissible under UCC § 2-202.\n\n(C) is incorrect because it admits the contradictory term and excludes the consistent additional term, which is the exact opposite of the parol evidence rule.\n\n(D) is incorrect because consistent additional terms are admissible to supplement a partially integrated agreement."
    },
    {
        id: 21,
        topic: "Parol Evidence Rule / Condition Precedent",
        fp: "Buyer, Inc., contracted in writing with Shareholder, who owned all of XYZ Corporation's outstanding stock, to purchase all of her stock at a specified price per share. At the time this contract was executed, Buyer's contracting officer said to Shareholder, 'Of course, our commitment to buy is conditioned on our obtaining approval of the contract from Conglomerate, Ltd., our parent company.' Shareholder replied, 'Fine. No problem.' Later, Shareholder was willing and ready to consummate the sale of her stock to Buyer, but the latter refused to perform on the ground (which was true) that Conglomerate had firmly refused to approve the contract.",
        q: "If Shareholder sues Buyer for breach of contract and seeks to exclude any evidence of the oral condition requiring Conglomerate's approval, the court will probably",
        opts: [
            "admit the evidence as proof of a collateral agreement.",
            "admit the evidence as proof of a condition to the existence of an enforceable obligation, and therefore not within the scope of the parol evidence rule.",
            "exclude the evidence on the basis of a finding that the parties' written agreement was a complete integration of their contract.",
            "exclude the evidence as contradicting the terms of the parties' written agreement, whether or not the writing was a complete integration of their contract."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the parol evidence rule does not bar extrinsic evidence showing that a written agreement was subject to an oral condition precedent to its legal effectiveness.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217, where the parties to a written agreement agree orally that performance of the agreement is subject to the occurrence of a condition precedent, the agreement is not effective as a contract until the condition occurs, and the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of that oral condition. The rationale is that the evidence is not offered to contradict or vary the terms of an existing contract, but rather to show that an enforceable contract never came into existence in the first place. Buyer and Shareholder orally agreed before signing that Buyer's commitment was conditioned on obtaining approval from Conglomerate. Because Conglomerate refused to approve the contract, the oral condition precedent to the existence of an enforceable obligation failed, and evidence of that oral condition is admissible.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the condition went to the effectiveness of the entire contract, rather than being a separate, collateral contract supported by distinct consideration.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the condition-precedent exception to the parol evidence rule applies even if the written contract appears completely integrated on its face.\n\n(D) is incorrect because an oral condition precedent to contract formation or effectiveness is not regarded as an impermissible contradiction of the writing."
    },
    {
        id: 22,
        topic: "Terms / Interpretation (Ambiguity and Trade Usage)",
        fp: "Responding to County's written advertisement for bids, Tyres was the successful bidder for the sale of tires to County for County's vehicles. Tyres and County entered into a signed, written agreement that specified, 'It is agreed that Tyres will deliver all tires required by this agreement to County, in accordance with the attached bid form and specifications, for a one-year period beginning September 1, 2005.' Attached to the agreement was a copy of the bid form and specifications. In the written advertisement to which Tyres had responded, but not in the bid form, County had stated, 'Multiple awards may be issued if they are in the best interests of County.' No definite quantity of tires to be bought by County from Tyres was specified in any of these documents. In January 2006, Tyres learned that County was buying some of its tires from one of Tyres's competitors. Contending that the Tyres-County agreement was a requirements contract, Tyres sued County for the damages caused by County's buying some of its tires from the competitor.",
        q: "If County defends by offering proof of the advertisement concerning the possibility of multiple awards, should the court admit the evidence?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because the provision in the written agreement, 'all tires required by this agreement,' is ambiguous.",
            "Yes, because the advertisement was in writing.",
            "No, because of the parol evidence rule.",
            "No, because it would make the contract illusory."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "(A) is the best response, because extrinsic evidence is always admissible to explain or clarify an ambiguous term in a written contract.\n\nUnder UCC § 2-202 and general contract interpretation rules, the parol evidence rule does not bar extrinsic evidence offered to explain or interpret the meaning of ambiguous contract language. The phrase 'all tires required by this agreement' is facially ambiguous: it could mean an exclusive requirements contract for all tires the County actually needs for its fleet during the year, or it could mean only those tires that the County chooses to order pursuant to this specific contract. Because the phrase is susceptible to more than one reasonable interpretation, extrinsic evidence—including the County's bid advertisement stating that 'multiple awards may be issued'—is admissible to explain what the parties intended by the ambiguous wording.\n\n(B) is incorrect because whether extrinsic evidence is in writing or oral is not the legal test for resolving contractual ambiguity.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence offered to interpret or clarify ambiguous contract language.\n\n(D) is incorrect because admitting the advertisement does not render the agreement illusory; it aids the court in determining whether the parties intended an exclusive requirements contract or a series of standing offers."
    },
    {
        id: 23,
        topic: "Parol Evidence Rule / Course of Dealing",
        fp: "A buyer and a seller entered into a written contract for the sale of a copy machine, using the same form contract that they had used a number of times in the past. The contract stated that payment was due 30 days after delivery and provided that the writing contained the complete and exclusive statement of the parties' agreement. On several past occasions, the buyer had taken a 5% discount from the contract price when paying within 10 days of delivery, and the seller had not objected. On this occasion, when the buyer took a 5% discount for paying within 10 days, the seller objected because his profit margin on this particular machine was smaller than on his other machines.",
        q: "If the seller sues the buyer for breach of contract, may the buyer introduce evidence that the 5% discount was a term of the agreement?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the seller timely objected to the buyer's proposal for different terms.",
            "No, because the writing contained the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties.",
            "Yes, because a modification made in good faith does not require consideration.",
            "Yes, because evidence of course of dealing is admissible even if the writing contains the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because under UCC § 2-202(a), evidence of course of dealing is admissible to explain or supplement even a completely integrated written contract.\n\nUnder UCC § 2-202(a), terms set forth in a writing intended by the parties as a final expression of their agreement, and even terms in a complete and exclusive integration (merger clause), may be explained or supplemented by course of dealing, usage of trade, or course of performance. Under UCC § 1-303(b), a 'course of dealing' is a sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between the parties which is fairly to be regarded as establishing a common basis of understanding for interpreting their expressions and other conduct. The parties had executed identical form contracts in the past, and on several occasions the buyer took a 5% discount for paying within 10 days without objection from the seller. Because course of dealing shows what the parties understood their standard form contract terms to mean, evidence of this established payment practice is admissible to supplement the agreement, notwithstanding the merger clause.\n\n(A) is incorrect because the discount was established by an existing course of dealing, not a newly submitted proposal under UCC § 2-207.\n\n(B) is incorrect because UCC § 2-202 explicitly allows course of dealing to supplement a complete and exclusive writing.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the buyer is proving an existing implied term derived from course of dealing, rather than a post-formation modification."
    },
    {
        id: 24,
        topic: "Performance / Duty to Cooperate & Satisfy Conditions",
        fp: "A seller entered into a contract to sell to a buyer a house for a price of $150,000. The contract contained the following clause: 'This contract is conditional on the buyer's securing bank financing at an interest rate of 7% or below.' The buyer did not make an application for bank financing and therefore did not secure it, and refused to proceed with the purchase. The seller sued the buyer for breach of contract.",
        q: "Is the seller likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the buyer did not secure bank financing.",
            "No, because the contract did not expressly impose on the buyer any obligation to apply for bank financing.",
            "Yes, because a court will excuse the condition to avoid a disproportionate forfeiture.",
            "Yes, because a court will imply a term imposing on the buyer a duty to use reasonable efforts to secure bank financing."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because the law implies a duty of good faith requiring the buyer to make reasonable efforts to secure the required financing, and failure to make those efforts excuses the condition.\n\nEvery contract imposes upon each party an implied duty of good faith and fair dealing in its performance and enforcement (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 205). Where a party's duty is subject to a condition precedent whose fulfillment requires that party's cooperation, the court will imply a promise that the party will use reasonable, good-faith efforts to cause the condition to occur. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 245, where a party's breach of duty contributes materially to the non-occurrence of a condition, the non-occurrence of the condition is legally excused under the doctrine of prevention. Because the buyer made no application for financing, he breached his implied obligation to use reasonable efforts, preventing the condition from being fulfilled. As a result, the financing condition is excused, and the seller will prevail in his action for breach.\n\n(A) is incorrect because a party cannot rely on the failure of a condition precedent where the party's own bad-faith inaction caused the condition to fail.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the duty to use reasonable efforts to seek financing is implied by law as part of the covenant of good faith.\n\n(C) is incorrect because the condition is excused under the doctrine of prevention due to the buyer's breach, not under equitable forfeiture principles."
    },
    {
        id: 25,
        topic: "Performance / Waiver of Condition",
        fp: "In a writing signed by both parties on December 1, a buyer agreed to buy from a seller a gasoline engine for $1,000, delivery to be made on the following February 1. Through a secretarial error, the writing called for delivery on March 1, but neither party noticed the error until February 1. Before signing the agreement, the buyer and seller orally agreed that the contract of sale would be effective only if the buyer should notify the seller in writing not later than January 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the engine to a third person. Otherwise, they agreed orally, 'There is no deal.' On December 15, the buyer entered into a contract with a third person to resell the engine to the third person at a profit. On December 16, the buyer notified the seller by telephone of the buyer's resale agreement with the third person, and explained that a written notice was unfeasible because the buyer's secretary was ill. The seller replied, 'That's okay. I'll get the engine to you on February 1, as we agreed.' Having learned, however, that the engine had increased in value about 75% since December 1, the seller renounced the agreement on February 1.",
        q: "If the buyer sues the seller on February 2 for breach of contract, which of the following concepts best supports the buyer's claim?",
        opts: [
            "Substantial performance.",
            "Nonoccurrence of a condition subsequent.",
            "Waiver of condition.",
            "Novation of buyers."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the seller's statement 'That's okay' operated as a waiver of the condition requiring written notice of resale by January 2.\n\nA condition is an event that must occur before performance becomes due. A condition precedent may be waived by the words or conduct of the party for whose benefit the condition was created. Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 84 and UCC § 2-209, a party who is entitled to the benefit of a condition may waive it by manifesting an intention to perform despite the non-occurrence of the condition, and such a waiver is binding without consideration where the condition was not a material part of the agreed exchange. The requirement of a written notice by January 2 was a condition precedent to the seller's duty to sell. When the buyer gave timely oral notice on December 16 and the seller stated 'That's okay. I'll get the engine to you on February 1, as we agreed,' the seller expressly waived the requirement that the notice be in writing. The seller cannot later retract that waiver after the January 2 notice deadline has passed, because the buyer relied on the waiver by not sending written notice. Therefore, waiver of condition best supports the buyer's claim.\n\n(A) is incorrect because substantial performance applies to constructive conditions in construction and services contracts, not to express procedural conditions in the sale of goods.\n\n(B) is incorrect because notice of resale was a condition precedent to the contract's effectiveness, not a condition subsequent discharging an already existing duty.\n\n(D) is incorrect because a novation requires the substitution of a new party to the contract, which did not occur here."
    }
];